  "text": "We are given the function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\arctan(x^2) + \\arctan(x^3)\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find the **range** of this function, expressed in **interval notation**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the domain and behavior of the function\n\n- The function is defined for all real numbers $ x $, since $ x^2 $ and $ x^3 $ are defined for all real $ x $, and $ \\arctan $ is defined for all real inputs.\n- So the **domain** of $ f(x) $ is $ (-\\infty, \\infty) $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Analyze the range of each term\n\nWe know:\n\n- $ \\arctan(x) $ has a **range** of $ \\left( -\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right) $\n- But since $ x^2 \\geq 0 $ and $ x^3 $ can be positive or negative depending on $ x $, we'll consider the **positive** and **negative** cases.\n\n#### Case 1: $ x \\geq 0 $\n\n- Then $ x^2 \\geq 0 $, so $ \\arctan(x^2) \\in \\left[ 0, \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right) $\n- $ x^3 \\geq 0 $, so $ \\arctan(x^3) \\in \\left[ 0, \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right) $\n\nSo the sum:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\arctan(x^2) + \\arctan(x^3) \\in \\left[ 0, \\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right) = [0, \\pi)\n$$\n\n#### Case 2: $",
